WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:07.840
Jarosław 'pashaBiceps' Jarząbkowski was
born on April 11th, 1988 and grew up in Nasielsk,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.140
Poland — a village an hour away from Warsaw.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:15.520
The town was small and didn’t have many
things to do, so when an internet cafe opened

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.519
up nearby, pasha and his friends jumped at
the opportunity to pass the time playing video

00:00:20.519 --> 00:00:21.630
games.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:26.539
From this humble start, pasha stands today
as not only one of the most celebrated players

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.600
in the world, but also one of the most successful.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.060
He is a beloved community figure and a role
model for a generation of Counter-Strike fans.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.200
This is his story.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.580
After discovering Counter-Strike 1.6, pasha
quickly became the best player in his town.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.910
He began to play in tournaments and invested
plenty of time into the game, further growing

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.000
his skills.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.490
However, his passion wasn’t enough to put
bread on the table — especially considering

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:01.920
that by the end of high school he was already
seriously committed to his future wife, Kinga

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.720
The scene was simply not yet big enough to
play Counter-Strike full-time, so pasha made

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.280
ends meet by working construction jobs.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.820
When Kinga found work in the big city of Warsaw,
the pair moved there, but quickly found it

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.890
difficult to cope with the increased cost
of living.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:21.780
Pasha tried to study physical therapy, but
left his education after half a year — the

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.030
lure of Counter-Strike was too strong, and
he wasn’t ready to give up on his dream

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.070
of playing professionally.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:32.860
With Kinga at his side, pasha decided to make
a gamble and play Counter-Strike full-time,

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.330
promising her that someday the risk would
be worth it.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:40.990
Pasha devoted himself to the game and year
after year he made more and more money.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:46.120
He gained the attention of some of Poland’s
greatest CS players, collectively known as

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:51.409
“The Golden Five”, including TaZ, NEO,
Loord and kuben.

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:56.420
This already legendary group recruited pasha
into their ranks, launching his career to

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.570
the next level.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:02.150
Alongside those players, pasha’s dream began
to become a reality.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:07.000
For the next few years, pasha and his Polish
brethren were an elite force in the CS 1.6

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.619
scene — especially in 2011.

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:14.190
The Poles were threats at any event they attended,
and continued to perform all the way until

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:17.490
the switch to Counter-Strike: Global Offensive
in late 2012.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.840
But, the change in version wasn’t easy.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:25.060
Pasha and the team were still top-tier competitors,
but their performance fluctuated dramatically

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.200
throughout the back half of 2012 and early
2013.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.040
While they had a number of Top 4 finishes,
they placed badly just as often.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:38.800
Despite sticking together for many years,
it was time for a change, and so Loord and

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.870
kuben would leave the team in the later half
of 2013.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.709
During this difficult time, pasha and Kinga
were married.

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:49.970
This big step cast doubt into pasha’s mind,
and he questioned whether or not he should

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.180
leave Counter-Strike behind for a more “normal”
job.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.560
His teammates NEO and TaZ talked him into
sticking with the team, predicting that the

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.780
scene would grow and he would be able to support
his wife by playing.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:22.220
With the empty slots on the roster filled
with the young talents of Snax and byali,

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.379
pasha continued to chase his dream.

00:03:24.379 --> 00:03:31.290
Unfortunately, at the first CS:GO Major, DreamHack
Winter 2013, the new roster got off to a slow

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.660
start, failing to make it out of the group
stage.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:49.200
Inconsistency, it seemed, was their consistency,
a trend that holds true to this day.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.000
Some days, the team were an unstoppable juggernaut, and other days they were limp and listless,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:12.680
(Commentators Casting)

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:17.370
Thankfully, they wasted no time in bouncing
back, and in front of their hometown Polish

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:23.440
crowd, won the next major, ESL One Katowice
2014, just five months later.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.580
The win heralded a period of strength and one

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:42.240
of their best years to date, and in 2014 they
had 14 Top-4 finishes at significant events.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:47.700
In-game, pasha found a home on the AWP, but
he also cemented himself as a flexible player

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.840
with unshakeable calm and experience to boot,
deadly in any situation.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:59.580
With his professional career an in-game skills
reaching new heights, pasha’s profile out

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.560
of game was also skyrocketing.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:06.580
His open personality and immensely popular
stream helped the world see that he is far

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.860
from your stereotypical gamer.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:29.400
He is a family man and an avid gym-goer, the
origin of the muscular moniker in his name.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:30.880
He earned respect for his friendly ways,

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.940
his passion for the game, his unwavering dedication

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:39.280
and love for his wife and newborn child.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.540
This combination of his deadly in-game play
and his genuine personality gained pasha a

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:55.160
very special spot in the hearts of the CS
community.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:57.630
He was becoming more than just another player.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:01.980
As the calendar turned to 2015, the inconsistent Virtus.pro returned.

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.220
But they still had some highlights - classic Virtus.pro.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:07:04.880
(Commentators Casting)

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:10.319
Their nine Top-4 finishes at premiere events
between March and July, including the semifinals

00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:16.919
exits at the Majors in Katowice and Cologne,
were balanced by 5 placings of 5-6th or worse

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:19.530
at premiere events during the same time period.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:24.889
For much of the fall Virtus.pro played extremely
well, but a disappointing loss in the quarterfinals

00:07:24.889 --> 00:07:29.520
of the DreamHack Cluj-Napoca Major was the
start of a slump by the Poles’ incredibly

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.560
high standards.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:43.280
Between October 2015 and May 2016 they bombed
out of the ESL ESEA Pro League Season 2, IEM

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:50.210
San Jose, DreamHack Leipzig, the IEM Masters
World Championship and DreamHack Masters Malmo

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:54.160
to add to another quarterfinal exit at the
MLG Major Championship: Columbus.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:10.240
(Commentators Casting)

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:15.160
There were issues in shotcalling and play.
However, the team did what they have always

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:18.050
done best: stick it out through thick and thin.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:22.910
Loyalty had always defined the Poles, and
here it shone through — although the community

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:26.910
speculated about a potential split, Virtus.pro
fought through it all.

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:31.490
A resurgence halfway through the year at the
ESL One Cologne Major followed by trophies

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:32.940
at ELEAGUE Season 1,

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:42.800
DreamHack Bucharest 2016,
DreamHack Masters Las Vegas 2017 and a hard-fought

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:48.139
second place at the ELEAGUE Major 2017 had
returned the team to their rightful place

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:51.100
as one of the elite teams in all of CS:GO.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:02.900
While they famously still struggle online,
no one dares mess with Virtus.pro on LAN.

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:21.700
(Commentators Casting)

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:26.000
As for pashaBiceps, he shows no sign of slowing down.

00:09:26.009 --> 00:09:32.160
Now 29 years old, older than most esport athletes,
his stream is more popular than ever and his

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:34.230
team is performing at the top level.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:39.029
While he dreams of some day opening a gym
or an esports academy of sorts in his native

00:09:39.029 --> 00:09:42.399
Poland, for now the ride isn’t over just yet.

00:09:42.399 --> 00:09:45.910
Oh, and that promise he made to his wife all
those years ago?

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:51.209
With more than 500,000 US dollars in prize
winnings (not to mention his impressive salary),

00:09:51.209 --> 00:09:53.180
it’s safe to say he kept his word.

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:08.230
Despite the struggles, pasha never doubted
in his team, his commitment to his family

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:10.879
or his dream, and it paid off.

00:10:10.879 --> 00:10:13.019
He truly is pashaBiceps.

